.TH "COMPRESS.SH" "1" "November 2026" "arran4/dotfiles" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
compress.sh \- create a compressed archive using its filename extension
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBcompress.sh ARCHIVE FILE...\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Choose tar, zip or rar based on ARCHIVE suffix and archive the remaining FILE arguments. Recognised suffixes: .tar, .tar.bz2, .tar.xz, .tar.gz, .tgz, .zip and .rar. Unrecognised suffixes currently produce no diagnostic.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
compress.sh backup.tar.gz Documents/Projects
compress.sh files.zip file1 file2
.fi
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Requires tar for tar archives, zip for zip files and rar for rar
files. Source paths must be readable. The helper does not add
recursive options for zip or rar: directories may require explicit
handling by their native utilities.
.SH ARCHIVE SELECTION
The first argument names the output archive. Its filename suffix
determines the archive program and compression mode. Subsequent
arguments are source files or directories, resolved relative to
the current directory unless given as absolute paths.
.SH ADDITIONAL EXAMPLES
.nf
compress.sh sources.tar.xz src README.md
compress.sh snapshot.tgz Documents/Projects
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
Unknown suffixes produce no error message and no archive. Existing
archive files may be overwritten or modified, depending on the
underlying program. This script does not verify the output archive;
do so before deleting input files.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Requires tar for tar formats, zip for .zip and rar for .rar. Input
paths must be readable. The helper does not add -r for zip or rar.
.SH HOW IT WORKS
The first argument is the output filename; its suffix selects the
archiver. Subsequent arguments are source files and directories,
resolved against the current directory unless absolute.
.SH ADDITIONAL EXAMPLES
.nf
compress.sh sources.tar.xz src README.md
compress.sh backup.tgz Documents/Projects
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
Unknown suffixes silently do nothing. Existing archives may be
overwritten or updated by the underlying utility. Verify the
resulting archive before deleting the input files.
.SH SEE ALSO
extract.sh(1)
